<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bryggen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bryggen is the old wharf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, lined with wooden merchant buildings. From around 1360 to 1754 it was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kontor">Hanseatic Kontor</a>, the German merchants' office that controlled the export of dried fish from northern Norway in exchange for grain. Rebuilt after many fires in its traditional form, it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bryggen.jpg" alt="Bryggen" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bryggen,_Bergen,_Noruega,_2019-09-08,_DD_115-117_PAN.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Brick Gothic churches, town halls and merchant houses gave the region a shared architectural style; the old towns of Lübeck, Visby, Stralsund and Wismar, Tallinn and the <b>Bryggen</b> wharf in Bergen are UNESCO World Heritage Sites.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>From the late thirteenth century German merchants of the Hanseatic League dominated this trade, and their Kontor at <b>Bryggen</b> in Bergen, established around 1360, controlled the exchange of fish for Baltic grain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…were carved on stone memorials, but also on wood, bone and metal for everyday purposes: more than six hundred runic inscriptions on sticks found at <b>Bryggen</b>, the old harbour of Bergen in Norway, from the 12th to the 15th centuries, include business letters, prayers, love messages and jokes.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-norse">Old Norse · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li></ul></section>
